in April the Bureau of Labor Statistics  issued its first jobs report since the  corona virus pandemic overwhelmed the US  the new data say that US employers shed  700 and 1,000 jobs in March change in  non-farm payrolls went negative for the  first time in 113 months the  unemployment rate also rose to four  point four percent from a 50-year low of  3.5% but it's still unlikely that these  losses show the full impact of the  crisis for the most recent jobs report  it's capturing the labor market before  the worst of the corona virus pandemic  took hold in the United States and  before many states acted to restrict  movement and close non-essential  businesses Laden March a different  metric initial jobless claims shot to  record highs then the next week six  point six million people filed claims  economists say that these numbers  foreshadow a coming surge in  unemployment here's how to read the  March jobs report in light of the  shutdowns  like nearly all such jobs reports the  March 1 was based on surveys that look  at the week that includes the 12th of  the month in March 2020 that was the 8th  through the 14th this month survey was  taken before many cities and states shut  down businesses to fight the spread of  the corona virus New York State  announced on March 20th  that non-essential businesses would  close that weekend more states and  cities issued orders throughout the week  and on March 30th governor's from  Maryland Washington DC and Virginia  issued stay at home orders to by April  1st the majority of states had issued  some version of a stay at home order if  the broad shutdowns that we're seeing  now continue we're gonna see a  staggering effect on the labor market  economists that I've spoke to have said  they expect as many as 20 million jobs  could be lost to put that into  perspective less than 9 million jobs are  lost during the Great Recession look  deeper into the report and you'll find  more clues about the virus's impact 1.8  million people reported being on  temporary layoff and average hours  worked  week four thirty four point two which  was down from thirty four point four in  February wages meanwhile ticked up and  the reason for that is job losses  expected to be concentrated among lower  wage workers people that work at  restaurants people that work at hotels  temporary hires is usually the first  area to slow down or fall when the  economy is at a point of stress the  report is also subject to unusual  statistical distortions we're not so  sure about the surveys that underlie  this because many businesses were closed  or no one was in the office and if no  one's there to give the response it's  possible that numbers were depressed and  we're not getting a accurate picture and  some of the people who lost their jobs  may be counted as out of the labor force  rather than unemployed because they're  not actively seeking work at a time when  the virus has shut businesses the April  jobs report will be released May 8th and  that's going to give us even greater  insight into the effect from the corona  virus pandemic  it will likely capture that hundreds of  thousands if not millions of Americans  have been laid off and that the  unemployment rate could be skyrocketing  some economists say well into the double  digits
 

